Answer: A default constructor is a no-argument constructor supplied by the compiler only when no constructor is declared in the class. If you declare any constructor, the compiler does not automatically add a no-argument constructor.

```java
class Student {
int id;
String name;
}
public class Main {
public static void main(String[] args) {
Student s = new Student();
System.out.println(s.id + " " + s.name);
}
}
```

Output: 0 null The compiler supplies a no-argument constructor. The instance variables receive their default values: 0 for int and null for String.

