Answer: A copy constructor is a constructor that accepts another object of the same class and copies its field values into a new object. It is useful when you want to preserve an object’s current state before modifying it. Java does not automatically provide a copy constructor; you define it yourself.

```java
class Customer {
int id;
String password;
```

Customer(int id, String password) {

```java
this.id = id;
this.password = password;
}
```

Customer(Customer ref) {

```java
this.id = ref.id;
this.password = ref.password;
}
}
```

Answer: The copied object has its own field values. Changing its fields does not change the original object’s primitive fields or immutable String references.

```java
class Customer {
int id;
String password;
```

Customer(int id, String password) {

```java
this.id = id;
this.password = password;
}
```

Customer(Customer ref) {

```java
this.id = ref.id;
this.password = ref.password;
}
}
public class Main {
public static void main(String[] args) {
Customer c1 = new Customer(1111, "2222");
Customer copy = new Customer(c1);
copy.id = 4321;
copy.password = "3333";
System.out.println(c1.id + " " + c1.password);
System.out.println(copy.id + " " + copy.password);
}
}
```

Output: 1111 2222 4321 3333 The copy constructor creates a new object and copies the original values into it. Later changes affect only the copied object.

