Answer: The following program reads customer details, creates a copy, modifies the original object, and restores its values from the copy.

```java
import java.util.Scanner;
class Customer {
int id;
String password;
```

Customer(int id, String password) {

```java
this.id = id;
this.password = password;
}
```

Customer(Customer ref) {

```java
this.id = ref.id;
this.password = ref.password;
}
```

void printDetails() {

```java
System.out.println(id + " " + password);
}
}
public class Main {
public static void main(String[] args) {
Scanner scan = new Scanner(System.in);
System.out.print("Enter id and password: ");
int id = scan.nextInt();
String password = scan.next();
Customer c1 = new Customer(id, password);
Customer copy = new Customer(c1);
c1.id = 4321;
c1.password = "3333";
System.out.println("Modified:");
c1.printDetails();
c1.id = copy.id;
c1.password = copy.password;
System.out.println("Restored:");
c1.printDetails();
scan.close();
}
}
```

Output: Enter id and password: 1111 2222 Modified: 4321 3333 Restored: 1111 2222 The copy constructor preserves the original values in a separate object. The values are then assigned back to the original object to restore its previous state.

